# Finding-level exceptions for the CI secret scan (.github/workflows/ci.yml).
# One line per confirmed false positive, identified by the exact fingerprint
# gitleaks 8.30.1 emits. Nothing here excuses a file, a commit, a rule, or a
# value shape -- only these three individual findings.
#
# All three are the same line in three copies of a Candidate47 freeze manifest:
#
#     "FounderDashboard/app/chatgpt-auth.ts": "4265a2e7...e42cc"
#
# The manifests are SHA-256 content hashes, one per file of the tree that was
# frozen, and Tools/Reliability250K/verify_frozen.py checks the tree against
# them. The value is the hash of that file, recomputed rather than assumed:
# sha256 of FounderDashboard/app/chatgpt-auth.ts is
# 4265a2e7c2dcb3a6f4b7026f762bf5b6e4caec76cc13c0a45987ee9a9f2e42cc, which is
# exactly the flagged value. generic-api-key fires on these three of the
# manifests' several hundred identically shaped lines because the key beside
# the hash is a path containing "auth".
#
# Editing the manifests is not an option: their bytes are the identity the
# verifier checks.
#
# A fingerprint pins the commit, the file, the rule and the line number, so
# any other finding in these same files -- including a real key on a
# neighbouring line -- is still reported. Measured both ways before this was
# committed; see Docs/Understanding/CANDIDATE47_RECONCILIATION.md section 10.
#
# SIX LINES, NOT THREE, BECAUSE THE FINGERPRINT PINS THE COMMIT.
#
# The same three findings are reachable at two different commits, and gitleaks
# scans history, so each needs its own line:
#
#   15bde21  Candidate47's own commit, reachable from
#            origin/codex/candidate47-closeout.
#   fc36db5  the squash of #106. A squash merge writes a NEW commit, so the
#            identical bytes arrived on main under a sha no entry named, and
#            main went red the moment it landed -- run 35231863437.
#
# Neither line is redundant while both commits are reachable. Before deleting
# either, check: `git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> origin/main` and
# `git branch -r --contains <sha>`. An entry matching nothing is invisible,
# not loud, which is the whole hazard here.
#
# The general rule this cost us: any future squash, rebase or amend that moves
# these manifests to a new commit needs its fingerprint added the same way.
# The falsifier is cheap -- the secret scan on main goes red and names the
# exact fingerprint to paste.
